```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Agent platform engineers who build two-stage (route-to-server-then-select-tool) MCP/tool routers that use a server-level editorial description (short blurb) for stage-1 routing instead of pooling member tool schemas; also retrieval researchers studying topological reachability ceilings in agentic tool registries at scale S>10.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only pending L1 validation on real MCP registries with dense encoders. The L0 gap of +0.467±0.013 at S=40,c=0.5 (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:7) is synthetic, lexical-only, and assumes editorial under-coverage; real systems that follow collection-selection theory (Kulkarni&Callan/Salton, COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:97-98) pool member tool schemas, yielding coverage c≈1 and eliminating the ceiling. The stronger-stage-1 arm is lexical IDF+expansion only (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:10); a dense encoder could infer capability semantically from related tokens, partially closing the gap — the packet explicitly flags this as unresolved killer #4 (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:16-17, 134).
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Router must be changed from editorial-blurb to pooled-member representations or flat per-tool retrieval; if existing frameworks (LangChain/LlamaIndex/MCP-Zero) already pool, no code change is needed — the "fix" is already standard practice. Requires real MCP registry crawl to confirm under-coverage regime (c<1) exists in production; if c≈1 in practice, the ceiling does not bite. Dense-encoder stage-1 may recover some misses, reducing the operational win. Migration cost: re-indexing server representations, larger stage-1 index if pooling, or abandoning two-stage for flat (higher QPS cost).]
RATIONALE: The packet's L0 is a clean synthetic demonstration (24 seeds/cell, T=8, S≤40, lexical cosine, COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:6-7) that a coverage-gap tool is structurally unreachable when its distinctive token is absent from the server's editorial aggregate (100% of 39,312 misses, COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:86-87). However, ecological validity is insufficient: (a) the "editorial AGGREGATE != member centroid" framing is distinct from collection-selection theory only if real routers use short editorial blurbs; Kulkarni&Callan/Salton and IVF/nprobe model cluster scores as MEMBER GEOMETRY (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:97-99), and the packet's own stress-test (a) asks whether real systems pool member tool schemas, making coverage~1 (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:15-16). No evidence in the packet that production MCP registries route on under-covered blurbs. (b) The load-bearing #4 discriminator uses lexical IDF+expansion only (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:10); the packet concedes that a real DENSE embedding encoder might defeat the absent-token condition via semantic inference (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:16-17, 134), and L1 is required to resolve this. (c) Ecological under-coverage (c<1) is assumed, not measured on a real ToolBench/MCP corpus (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:17). Without (a)-(c), the +0.467 gap is a microbenchmark artifact of the synthetic editorial-blurb regime, not a proven production ceiling. The negative result is actionable guidance — "do not route on editorial blurbs; pool member schemas or go flat" — but that guidance restates standard collection-selection practice. Per TOOLING_v001.md, novelty requires retrievable prior-art verification; the packet cites Tool-to-Agent (2511.01854), ToolRerank (2403.06551), MCP-Zero (2506.01056) as treating failures as geometry/ranking (COMMITTEE1_PACKET.md:96), but provides no citation that they use editorial blurbs with under-coverage. Hence YELLOW: mechanism holds in the synthetic regime, but operational significance is unproven pending L1 on real registries with dense encoders.
```
